edict whether uh the person's annual  income is more than or less than fifty  thousand dollars  so they uh i think they did like 500  different  uh iterations of the stochastic gradient  descent for that data set  and what they found was that um there's  a very high degree of variability  uh in the answers across the iterations  and  in some degree in some in some  iterations the  variability could be as high as 50 which  means  that your model predictions are no  better than just a coin flip  right and given that machine learning is  becoming more and more a part of  you know mainstream applications this  kind of  uncertainty in predictions cannot go  under just  and that is basically what they try to  do with the high grad algorithm  so it's sort of like a different  way of looking at things it's um sort of  a different paradigm  um so so far the  way we're all familiar with these  machine learning problems you have some  you know ground troop the actual value  of some data point  say in this case it's 6.9 your model  makes a prediction  uh for that value say the the model  predicts 6.4  right and this is usually what we'll be  working with  the actual value and which is y and the  y hat which is your mod what your model  predicts  what the high grad algorithm does and  what the  authors propose and others have also  proposed in other ways is  um the idea of attaching confidence  intervals  to these predictions um so instead of  just saying  that the model thinks the answer is 6.4  the model will say that i think the  answers between 5.5 and 7.3  with a 95 confidence um  and this you know is that that  confidence interval and the length of  that interval is  a quantification of the uncertainty of  that model's prediction  if it's a very wide interval you know  that this model isn't very confident  about what it's predicting  if it's a fairly tight interval you can  be more confident about  uh the predictions of the model now  given we're working with  confidence intervals one term that you  should be familiar with  is coverage probability now it's a very  fancy  term to um denote something fairly  simple  when you're working with confidence  intervals you're going to specify an  interval for the values  and whether that value whether the true  value  falls within that confidence interval or  not  is basically you know the  it's basically the success of failure of  that confidence interval the coverage  probability  is essentially the probability that this  happens so over multiple iterations  it tells you whether um the  true value falls within the confidence  interval that you've proposed  so in this image you can see so there's  like a lot of uh data points on the  screen  and the ones in yellow so there's like  the  the point itself you could think of as  the prediction and then the  uh the wicks that you see uh above and  below the point  are like the confidence interval and  basically all of these predictions are  for  the zero uh mean uh  of data right they're just all trying to  predict zero  effectively so all the ones that are  yellow um  essentially do include zero in it in the  confidence interval  so they're all um like uh good  so they're correct however the blue  points do not include the zero so  those all of those confidence intervals  are wrong and  say if you're correct 95 of the time the  coverage probability would be 95  or 0.95 so that's a term to be familiar  with  um given we're working with confidence  intervals now and is basically a test  for  how well those confidence intervals work  and is one of the metrics that the  authors even use in the paper to judge  how well the high grad algorithm and  certain configurations of it do  the other one that they use is called  informativeness um  which is basically a measure of the  length of the confidence interval for  example  in this case 5.5 and 7.3 so uh that's  like 1.8  is the length of this interval um and if  the interval interval's wider  the prediction doesn't really have much  information in it right so if you know  the  answers are going to vary the answer is  usually going to vary between 0  and 10 and the model spits out a  confidence interval that that  says you know i think with 95 percent  confidence that the answer is going to  be between 0 and 10 it doesn't really  tell you much  so that's basic however if it says the  answer is probably going to be between  5.5 and 5.9 then this is like a far more  informative prediction  than just saying it's between 0 and 10.  so  another metric to keep in mind uh that  the author is also used in the paper  so now we get to the actual algorithm  and the  associated python library  the algorithm called hierarchical  incremental gradient descent  it has this very fancy logo um which i  i'm pretty sure is unofficial  it was on the github page of the  r library that they have for the high  grad algorithm and i really liked it so  i  kept it and i put it on my github page  also for the high grad pi  so and it actually very well represents  what  happens in the algorithm basically the  prongs of the trident  represent um the different threads that  the sgde splits into  so this is the high grad algorithm  itself  formerly like written down in pseudocode  i won't go into the math behind the  algorithm  as much simply because i feel the  authors can probably explain the math  much better than i can  so if you want to learn um what happens  under the hood  i would uh point you to the 50 page  research paper that they have on the  subject  and like the 100 plus slide presentation  that one of the authors has on the  subject  so that's where you should go if you  want to thoroughly understand what's  going on with the algorithm  my objective here is going to be uh  giving you  a more uh intuitive overview of what  happens  and then showcasing the library itself  so this is what um  happens uh in the high grad algorithm it  starts out  the same as any other stochastic  gradient descent so the hybrid basically  builds upon  so incremental gradient descent is  basically stochastic gradient descent  so that's the same as what we've already  discussed in your what you're familiar  with  the hierarchical part is where the  novelty lies  so it starts out the same as  the usual stochastic gradient descent  and  up to some you know uh number of  iterations  post which it splits into multiple  threads  right and the number of threads that  will split into is  a hyper parameter that you choose even  the number of steps it will  process as a single singular uh thread  before it  creates the first split is also a hyper  parameter  and um those i'll get to in a bit  so basically it starts out as the usual  stochastic gradient descent and then  splits into  two or more threads right and now so  this is like say level  uh so we were at level one now there's  level two  of this um of the algorithms training  now these individual threads can further  split into more threads  um maybe they split into two three four  or more  and could be they both split into the  same number or different number of  threads further  and all of these are hyper parameters  that we set um  for the algorithm and now what happens  is so there are multiple levels and  so basically these are the hierarchy uh  across  um the training that's going on so  there's level one where you had just one  thread  then level two where it's split into two  or more and then when each of them  splits into furthermore  you know you have level three and four  and so on  so this is um  where things get found so what happens  instead of uh  taking like so how do the things change  first up how does the gradient step  change  so instead of the gradient step being  you know just one singular  um instance um it's now multiple threads  where each of them has some um you know  gradient step as per its own thread um  and we take the weighted average across  those uh  within the same thread so for each step  within the thread  and then we take the average across all  threads  right so i don't know if this uh diagram  is making sense to you or not  so if we just look at you know from left  to right  the first um the first piece so to speak  the second piece uh that is the one  above it and the third piece that's the  one above that  so these three threads um  so they're not really three threads it's  basically uh the same thread um  with three different pieces so we take  the weighted  average of the first piece the second  piece and the  third piece um the respective gradients  and  this weighted average is then averaged  across  all the different threads the four  threads that we have uh in front of us  um the four leaves so to speak  right so this is basically a tree and  those end points are the leaves  we take the average across those and  that is going to be the gradient step  so um much much more fancy  than the standard the vanilla stochastic  gradient descent  how the inference changes um which is  basically how does the model make a  prediction  instead of uh giving you its usual  prediction it will now  run a prediction through each one of the  threads and will take the average of all  them  and using those it will also  uh and again these are non-trivial  in the sense that it's not um you know  it's not like  just some basic computation it uh  includes like the correlations and the  covariances and the standard errors  and then creates like a confidence  interval um  for the prediction uh above and below  the estimate so there is  so each time when you ask the model for  a prediction  it not only gives you a prediction  itself it also attaches a confidence  interval to it  um yeah so  another thing to understand um  within the algorithm and this is like  the the two more uh  the two most important hyper parameters  um of  the high grad algorithm um  and the id the underlying ideas being  contrasting and sharing  um contrasting is the idea of  having many different threads in the  training  and in the model which all contrast  you know um the different values  right so each one of them will give you  a different prediction for the same date  uh  for the same uh data point and then  contrasting these uh you'll uh  you'll construct the confidence interval  sharing is the idea of sharing the  gradients across these different threads  and then computing the average to take a  step  uh with the gradient descent step so  contrasting requires more and more  threads  and so the two  hyper parameters here are how long do  you wait before you split  or you start splitting into threads and  how many threads just  does each uh branch split into so  the more contrasting you have the less  sharing there will be  because when you have like a lot of  threads um  and there's a lot of gradients so  there's not much weight  to each gradient however when you try to  increase sharing in which case you have  lower  um like lower and the lesser number of  threads and longer threads  you'll have lesser contrasting also so  this is  uh where you have to strike the balance  uh and we're back to that goldilocks uh  situation that we did with say the  learning rate  um or you could think of you know say  the bias variance tradeoff  so contrasting and sharing is another  one of those um  tug-of-war problems where you know  getting more of one means  less of the other so  yeah this is basically uh something to  be aware of  and i'll show you an example with the  data  uh that shadows that better demonstrates  this  visually um yeah so  that that that was basically the  overview of the algorithm itself  um and what does it effectively achieve  right  so as the pred as a practitioner what do  you care about  number one um it retains the online  nature of the vanilla stochastic radiant  descent  so that's a plus and the best part is  it's  at the same computational cost so even  though my explanation of it  or you know on an intuitive level it  might seem like  because there's a lot more going on this  is going to be computationally more  expensive  however it's not it's um it  the computational cost is the same as a  vanilla stochastic gradient descent so  essentially all this additional  information about the confidence  interval that you get  is for free right computationally  speaking  so and everyone likes free stuff right  so  that's a good thing uh number two  instead of just having the estimator  which was like  the which is like the prediction that  you would get out of the vanilla  stochastic gradient descent  you get the estimator plus you get a  confidence interval attached to that  estimate  so that's number two and number three is  that the estimator that you would get  is uh almost as accurate as just the  vanilla stochastic gradient descent  right so you aren't really losing out on  anything and you're getting  the confidence interval information and  also  as a bonus the high grad algorithm is  far easier to parallelize than the  vanilla stochastic gradient  centers so that's another plus  now getting to the library itself it's  um already uploaded to  uh pipey or pi pi depending on how you  pronounce it  so you can do a pip install high grad pi  and fetch it  so we're currently on version 0.17  the picture on screen is outdated  so you can easily install the library  and with the library itself the api i've  tried to keep it as  similar to cycle learn as possible  because um  if you have any experience or even if  you've done any basic course  on uh machine learning with python  you've  for sure been introduced to the  scikit-learn library  and um the scikit-learn api is like  the standard right that you create the  model  uh you do model dot fit and you do  modern dot predict to get the answer  so the high grad pi api works in the  exact same way  you import the model from the high grad  pile library  you create the model object you do model  fit  you do modern predict and you're done  so n